cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use ATR in academic work or security research, please cite it as below."
title: "ATR: Agent Threat Rules — Open Detection Standard for AI Agent Threats"
abstract: >
  ATR is an open-source detection rule set for AI agent threats, analogous to
  Sigma for SIEM or YARA for malware. At v4.1.5 it provides 823 rules across 10
  threat categories (prompt injection, tool poisoning, context exfiltration,
  agent manipulation, skill compromise, model abuse, data poisoning, privilege
  escalation, excessive autonomy, model security), mapped to OWASP Agentic Top
  10 (10/10 categories), SAFE-MCP (78 of 85 techniques) and MITRE ATLAS. Rules
  are hand-authored from real attack payloads, including the NVIDIA garak probe
  corpus, and are held to a community safety gate that requires true-positive
  and true-negative test cases on every rule, verifies each rule matches its own
  true positives, and rejects any rule that fires on the committed benign skill
  corpus. The exact thresholds are defined in scripts/check-rules-safety.ts and
  CONTRIBUTING.md rather than here.
  On the 650-prompt garak in-the-wild jailbreak corpus, measured at ATR 4.1.5,
  recall is 92.3% in the hunt lane (the engine default, which loads every rule
  maturity) and 0.9% in the enforce lane (stable rules only, the lane to run
  with blocking on). The hunt figure is partly closed-book: many of the rules
  that fire on this corpus were written from it (README section 8).
  Rule counts change daily. Current counts, per-corpus benchmark figures and the
  measurement file behind each one are published in data/stats.json and the
  README evaluation table; those are the citable source, not this abstract.
